# Redoubt -- mozconfig
#
# Android counterpart of assets/mozconfig (== assets/mozconfig.new, the file
# scripts/librewolf-patches.py copies into the tree as ./mozconfig).
#
# Owner: LW-M0-03.  Every option below was checked against the extracted
# Firefox 153.0.4 source tree; the file:line the check came from is quoted in
# the comment next to it.  Re-check the quoted line numbers on the next Firefox
# rebase.
#
# This file was originally written with NO toolchain available, so every claim
# in it was static, against moz.configure sources.  That is no longer uniformly
# true: M1 built it (aarch64 GeckoView AAR + APK, ./mach build exit 0), so
# where a comment says "verified in the artefact" it means exactly that.
# Comments that still read as static reasoning have not been re-checked against
# a build; do not upgrade their confidence without doing so.
#
# LW-M5-03 re-derived the whole hardening block from three built objdirs
# (LW-M2-01's stock baseline and LW-M2-02's two patched trees) and from the
# aarch64 ELF they produced, measured every candidate addition with the pinned
# NDK clang, added the two options below that close a real desktop/Android
# asymmetry (--enable-phc and -Wl,--rosegment), and re-built this file twice from
# a fresh stock 153.0.4 tree -- ./mach build exit 0 both times.  It also ran
# configure with --target=arm-linux-androideabi, --target=x86_64-linux-android and
# --target=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu, so the per-ABI and desktop numbers quoted below
# are measured rather than derived.  The findings, the residual gaps and the
# options that were considered and rejected are written up in
# docs/android/parity/hardening-flags.md.
#
# Mozilla's own working Android mozconfigs, used as the shape reference:
#   mobile/android/config/mozconfigs/common
#   mobile/android/config/mozconfigs/android-aarch64/{release,beta,nightly}
#   mobile/android/config/mozconfigs/common.override
# We deliberately do NOT source them: they hardcode taskcluster
# ($MOZ_FETCHES_DIR) paths, Mozilla API keyfiles and -fcrash-diagnostics-dir.
# This file is standalone, exactly like the desktop one.
#
# The "DROPPED / CHANGED FROM DESKTOP" block at the bottom is the summary that
# docs/android/parity/hardening-flags.md expands.  Keep both in sync when you
# edit this file.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application and target
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --enable-application is an accepted alias that implies --enable-project
# (build/moz.configure/init.configure:388-401, "Same as --enable-project").
# Mozilla's own mobile mozconfigs spell it --enable-project=mobile/android;
# both land in the same place, and --enable-application keeps this file
# line-for-line comparable with the desktop one.
ac_add_options --enable-application=mobile/android

# aarch64 is the primary (and, for M0-M4, only) target.  arm64-v8a is the only
# ABI worth shipping first; armv7/x86_64 come later via LW-M6-xx multi-arch.
# mobile/android/moz.configure:140-152 hard-dies unless target.os == "Android".
ac_add_options --target=aarch64-linux-android


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# app-services in tree  (REQUIRED -- consumed by LW-M2-05)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Verified spelling: toolkit/moz.configure:4525-4536 declares
#   option("--enable-appservices-in-tree", help="Enables building from an
#   in-tree app-services")
# and sets MOZ_APPSERVICES_IN_TREE.  The option name in 153 is exactly the one
# the board asked for -- no rename.  Consumers that read the subst:
#   moz.build:184, settings.gradle:36, mobile/android/fenix/settings.gradle:42,
#   mobile/android/android-components/settings.gradle:38,
#   mobile/android/focus-android/settings.gradle:41,
#   third_party/application-services/moz.build:5
#
# LW-M2-05 VERIFIED THIS AGAINST A BUILT ARTEFACT (the M1 GeckoView build in
# ~/lw-m2-01b, whose /work/mozconfig.android is byte-identical to this file).
# What the flag actually buys, and what it does NOT:
#
#   * config.status carries 'MOZ_APPSERVICES_IN_TREE': '1'; the build log shows
#     `Compiling remote_settings v0.1.0 (.../third_party/application-services/
#     components/remote_settings)` and the same for search, nimbus-fml,
#     toolchain-nimbus-fml and embedded-uniffi-bindgen.  Zero `org.mozilla.
#     appservices:*` maven artifacts are fetched.  `uniffi v0.31.0` builds from
#     the vendored third_party/rust copy (.cargo/config.toml.in replaces
#     crates.io with vendored-sources), so it is in-tree too.
#   * The concrete thing the flag prevents is a network fetch of the
#     *nimbus-fml tool*: NimbusGradlePlugin.kt:121 branches on the subst; the
#     else branch downloads nimbus-fml.zip from archive.mozilla.org (:267) or
#     firefox-ci-tc (:276).  With the flag on it uses the NIMBUS_FML subst,
#     which is $DIST/host/bin/nimbus-fml, built by services/moz.build:30-31
#     (Android-only, and NOT itself gated on this flag).
#   * It also swaps `org.mozilla.appservices:<mod>` maven coordinates for
#     project(':<mod>') -- but only under ProjectPlugin.kt:112's *second*
#     condition: MOZ_APPSERVICES_IN_TREE AND (DOWNLOAD_ALL_GRADLE_DEPENDENCIES
#     OR :geckoview is in this Gradle build).  A standalone
#     `cd mobile/android/fenix && gradlew ...` invocation has no :geckoview,
#     so the substitution silently does NOT happen and Fenix consumes Mozilla's
#     prebuilt app-services AARs while this flag is still set.  LW-M2-04 must
#     drive the APK from a Gradle build that includes :geckoview, or the
#     "no prebuilt artifact" acceptance criterion fails invisibly.
#
# NOT what the old comment here claimed.  The remote_settings dumps are ours to
# *edit* regardless of this flag: Cargo.toml:19-58 lists every app-services
# component as a workspace member unconditionally, and the dumps are pulled in
# by include_str!/include_bytes! (components/remote_settings/src/macros.rs:10,
# 28,56,65) from CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR.  What the flag decides is whether the
# *megazord* that contains them is built here (moz.build:184 ->
# third_party/application-services/moz.build:5 -> megazords/full +
# megazords/fenix-dylib).  Verified in the artefact: search-config-v2.json is
# embedded byte-for-byte in dist/geckoview/lib/arm64-v8a/libmegazord.so at
# offset 0x169150, all 7 packaged collections and the regions attachments are
# there, and none of them is in libxul.so.
#
# Where Android search config really comes from -- input for LW-M4-06:
#   Fenix -> SearchEngineSelectorRepository.kt -> mozilla.appservices.search
#   -> Rust `search` crate -> RemoteSettingsService -> the dumps compiled into
#   libmegazord.so.  services/settings/dumps/ never reaches Android at all:
#   services/settings/dumps/main/moz.build:19 excludes search-config-v2.json
#   for any MOZ_BUILD_APP starting with "mobile/", and the built omni.ja proves
#   it (its defaults/settings/ has only gfx, last_modified, doh-config,
#   doh-providers, password-recipes and onecrl).  So scripts/
#   librewolf-patches.py:366-372, which cp's assets/search-config-v2.json into
#   services/settings/dumps/main/, is inert on Android -- landmine L4's shape,
#   and it is the whole LibreWolf search config, not just add-mojeek.patch.
#
# Three mechanics LW-M4-06 has to get right when it writes the app-services
# side, all read out of components/remote_settings/src/client.rs:
#   * The dump is only preferred over cached/synced records when its timestamp
#     is NEWER (get_packaged_data_if_newer, :277-295).  A replacement dump
#     whose timestamp is not bumped past whatever the device already synced is
#     simply ignored -- it will look like the edit did nothing.
#   * Both the `timestamp` field inside the .json and the sibling .timestamp
#     file must be updated together; client.rs:249 debug_asserts they are equal.
#   * That whole branch is gated on is_prod_server() (:309), so a build pointed
#     at a non-prod remote-settings server never loads the packaged dump at all.
# And note that patches/rs-blocker.patch does NOT cover this path: it touches
# only services/settings/*.mjs plus SearchEngineSelector.sys.mjs, so
# librewolf.services.settings.allowedCollections has no effect on the Rust
# RemoteSettingsService that Fenix actually uses.  That is LW-M4-08's problem.
#
# MOZ_ESR does NOT change which search config the Android build resolves.
# LW-M0-07 flagged SearchUtils.sys.mjs:361 and
# RustSharedRemoteSettingsService.sys.mjs:44 as both selecting an "esr" channel,
# and version.android pins 153.0esr, so this looked like a real divergence.  It
# is not, for two independent reasons:
#   1. Both sites are Gecko-JS-only and unreachable on Android.
#      toolkit/components/moz.build:103-110 drops uniffi-js and
#      uniffi-bindgen-gecko-js/components for MOZ_BUILD_APP == "mobile/android",
#      and toolkit/library/rust/shared/Cargo.toml:135 /lib.rs:107-108 drop
#      gkrust-uniffi-components under cfg(not(target_os = "android")).  So
#      generated/RustSearch.sys.mjs and generated/RustRemoteSettings.sys.mjs do
#      not exist in the Android build -- confirmed by extracting the built
#      omni.ja: 2275 files, zero matching *uniffi*, while
#      SearchEngineSelector.sys.mjs:17-25, SearchService.sys.mjs:2809,
#      ConfigSearchEngine.sys.mjs and RustSharedRemoteSettingsService.sys.mjs
#      are all packaged and all import them.  SearchUtils.MODIFIED_APP_CHANNEL
#      has exactly one non-test consumer (SearchService.sys.mjs:2809) and that
#      code path cannot run.
#   2. The path that does run is Kotlin and never sees MOZ_ESR.  Fenix's
#      Components.kt:258 passes channel = BuildConfig.BUILD_TYPE to the Rust
#      RemoteSettingsService, and Core.kt:785-790/799-803 derives the search
#      updateChannel from Config.kt:47's ReleaseChannel, whose `when` accepts
#      only debug/nightly/benchmark/beta/release and throws on anything else.
#      There is no ESR value to reach, even though the Rust enum has one
#      (components/search/src/types.rs:43).  `grep -rn "MOZ_ESR\|IS_ESR"
#      mobile/android/` returns one hit and it is a unit test.
# For the record, had the channel been "esr" the delta would have been small:
# in Mozilla's shipped dump only bing/ddg/google carry an esr subVariant, and
# each changes only partnerCode + telemetrySuffix (MOZR, ftsa, firefox-b-e,
# firefox-b-1-e).  assets/search-config-v2.json has 92 records -- ddg,
# startpage, mojeek and 87 wikipedia* -- and contains the strings
# "partnerCode", "telemetrySuffix" and "esr" zero times each, so once LW-M4-06
# lands the equivalent replacement on the app-services side there is nothing
# for an esr channel to select.  LW-M4-06 therefore does not differ from
# desktop on this axis.
ac_add_options --enable-appservices-in-tree


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Toolchain locations -- these belong to the container image, NOT to this file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LW-M0-06 (assets/Dockerfile.android) owns installing and pinning the SDK,
# NDK, JDK and Gradle, and must export the variables read below.  Nothing here
# hardcodes a path.  Each block is guarded so that an unset variable falls back
# to configure's own default (~/.mozbuild lookup + `mach bootstrap`), instead
# of passing an empty --with-... that configure would reject.
#
# Hard requirement LW-M0-06 must satisfy, read out of the tree:
#   NDK  r29  -- python/mozboot/mozboot/android.py:28 (NDK_VERSION = "r29");
#                build/moz.configure/android-ndk.configure:113-121 dies with
#                "The only supported version of the NDK is %s" on a mismatch.
#   SDK  build-tools 37.0.0, compileSdk 37, targetSdk 36, minSdk 26
#                -- build/moz.configure/android-sdk.configure:152-161.
# mozboot also honours $ANDROID_NDK_HOME (android.py:46), hence the fallback.

if [ -n "$ANDROID_SDK_ROOT" ]; then
    ac_add_options --with-android-sdk="$ANDROID_SDK_ROOT"
fi

if [ -n "$ANDROID_NDK_ROOT" ]; then
    ac_add_options --with-android-ndk="$ANDROID_NDK_ROOT"
elif [ -n "$ANDROID_NDK_HOME" ]; then
    ac_add_options --with-android-ndk="$ANDROID_NDK_HOME"
fi

# build/moz.configure/java.configure:8-35.  Passing this explicitly is better
# than JAVA_HOME alone: configure logs "Ignoring JAVA_HOME value" when it
# auto-detects a different JDK.
if [ -n "$JAVA_HOME" ]; then
    ac_add_options --with-java-bin-path="$JAVA_HOME/bin"
fi

# mobile/android/gradle.configure:9-46.  With no --with-gradle, configure falls
# back to the in-tree ./gradlew wrapper, which downloads Gradle at build time.
# LW-M0-06 should set GRADLE_BIN to a pinned, pre-installed gradle so the build
# has no network dependency.
if [ -n "$GRADLE_BIN" ]; then
    ac_add_options --with-gradle="$GRADLE_BIN"
fi


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build type -- straight carry-over from assets/mozconfig
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ac_add_options --disable-debug
ac_add_options --disable-tests
ac_add_options --enable-optimize
ac_add_options --enable-release
# build/moz.configure/rust.configure:711
ac_add_options --disable-cargo-incremental

# Crash reporting.  Supported and properly conditionalised on Android:
# toolkit/moz.configure:3463-3479 (crashreporter_default has an explicit
# target.os == "Android" branch), and the Java side degrades cleanly --
# mobile/android/geckoview/build.gradle:70 emits the BuildConfig flag,
# GeckoRuntime.java:429 early-returns when it is false, and
# android-components/components/lib/crash/build.gradle:41 reads the same subst.
ac_add_options --disable-crashreporter

# Already the default for mobile/android -- build/moz.configure/
# update-programs.configure:8-19 returns False when build_project ==
# "mobile/android".  Kept explicit so the intent survives an upstream default
# flip; passing --disable on an already-False default is not an error.
ac_add_options --disable-updater


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HARDENING -- the whole point of this file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every hardening option from assets/mozconfig is below.  NONE were dropped.
#
# LW-M5-03 confirmed, from the built artefact rather than from this file, that
# each one is in effect on aarch64-linux-android.  The three Android objdirs
# (LW-M2-01 stock, LW-M2-02 patched 153.0.4 and patched 153.0esr) carry a
# byte-identical hardening block in config.status, and the ELF shows the
# mitigations really landed:
#   * 22 distinct fortified bionic entry points imported by libxul.so
#     (__memcpy_chk, __strcpy_chk, __vsnprintf_chk, __FD_SET_chk, ...)
#                                                      -> _FORTIFY_SOURCE=2 live
#   * __stack_chk_fail@LIBC imported; every non-leaf frame loads the cookie
#     from TPIDR_EL0                                   -> -fstack-protector-strong live
#   * _ZNSt6__ndk122__libcpp_verbose_abortEPKcz imported from libmozglue.so
#     -- libc++ only emits that call when hardening assertions are compiled in
#                                                      -> STL hardening live
#   * full RELRO + BIND_NOW, RW (not RWX) GNU_STACK, no TEXTREL
# The link-side set (-Wl,-z,{noexecstack,text,relro,now,nocopyreloc}) comes from
# build/moz.configure/flags.configure:358-362, gated only on
# building_with_gnu_compatible_cc -- identical on desktop clang and NDK clang.

# --enable-hardening: fully meaningful on Android.
# build/moz.configure/toolchain.configure:2665-2799.  On aarch64-linux-android
# with clang this yields, all of which bionic/NDK support:
#   -U_FORTIFY_SOURCE -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2   (requires --enable-optimize, set above)
#   -fstack-protector-strong                (cflags + ldflags)
#   -fstack-clash-protection                (:2720-2729 excludes only WINNT,
#                                            OSX, OpenBSD and iOS -- Android is
#                                            NOT excluded -- but on aarch64 it
#                                            additionally requires clang
#                                            >= 18.1.0.  RESOLVED by LW-M5-03:
#                                            the NDK r29 clang is 21.0.0, and
#                                            compiling a large-frame function
#                                            for aarch64-linux-android26 with
#                                            and without the flag differs by a
#                                            real probing loop
#                                            "sub sp,sp,#0x1000 / cmp sp,x9 /
#                                            str xzr,[sp] / b.ne" -- so this is
#                                            measured codegen, not an inferred
#                                            version comparison.  Note Mozilla
#                                            builds Android with their own
#                                            clang, not the NDK's -- see
#                                            mobile/android/config/mozconfigs/
#                                            common:18 pointing LD_LIBRARY_PATH
#                                            at $MOZ_FETCHES_DIR/clang/lib.
#                                            If LW-M0-06 ever downgrades clang
#                                            below 18.1.0 this silently stops
#                                            applying.)
#   THE ONE UNAVOIDABLE PER-ABI SPLIT, measured by LW-M5-03 rather than assumed:
#   on --target=arm-linux-androideabi (armeabi-v7a, the fat AAR's third ABI)
#   MOZ_HARDENING_CFLAGS comes out as
#     [-U_FORTIFY_SOURCE, -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2, -fstack-protector-strong,
#      -fstrict-flex-arrays=1]
#   -- NO -fstack-clash-protection, because the condition at :2723-2725 lists
#   x86/x86_64/ppc64/s390x (clang >= 11.0.1) and aarch64 (clang >= 18.1.0) and
#   32-bit "arm" appears in neither.  Nothing in this file can fix that; it is a
#   clang limitation.  x86_64-linux-android gets the full five, verified the same
#   way.
#   -fstrict-flex-arrays=1                  (clang >= 16.  Level 1 is the
#                                            desktop value and is deliberate:
#                                            :2749-2751 records that level 3
#                                            breaks the [0] GNU syntax the tree
#                                            uses everywhere and level 2 breaks
#                                            sqlite3 and icu.  Raising it on
#                                            Android alone would be a
#                                            divergence, not a parity fix.)
# The only target excluded wholesale at :2700 is WASI.
#
# NOT raised to -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=3, and that is not caution -- it is measured.
# bionic keys its object-size mode on the level being EXACTLY 2:
# sys/cdefs.h:240-247 sets __bos_level to 1 when _FORTIFY_SOURCE == 2 and to 0
# otherwise, so 3 falls into the *else* branch.  Compiled against the NDK r29
# sysroot, __builtin_object_size(s->a, __bos_level) for a char[8] member of a
# struct returns 8 at level 2 and -1 (unknown, i.e. unbounded) at both level 1
# and level 3.  On glibc, 3 is stronger; on bionic it is strictly weaker.
ac_add_options --enable-hardening

# --enable-stl-hardening: supported on Android, and NOT the default there.
# build/moz.configure/toolchain.configure:2802-2812 -- want_stl_hardening is
# `debug or target.os in ("WINNT", "OSX")`, so an Android release build gets
# NO STL hardening unless we ask.  This line is a real parity fix, not a no-op.
# :2837-2844 -- Android always uses libc++ (the NDK sysroot ships no libstdc++),
# so the using_libcxx branch is taken and the die("C++ STL does not support
# hardening") at :2851 is unreachable.  A non-debug build then gets either
#   -D_LIBCPP_HARDENING_MODE=_LIBCPP_HARDENING_MODE_EXTENSIVE   (libc++ >= 19)
# or the older
#   -D_LIBCPP_ENABLE_ASSERTIONS=1                               (libc++ < 19)
# -- both are real hardening, so this is safe either way.  RESOLVED by LW-M2-01
# and re-derived by LW-M5-03: we get the STRONGER branch.  All three Android
# objdirs carry
#   'MOZ_STL_HARDENING_FLAGS': ['-D_LIBCPP_HARDENING_MODE=_LIBCPP_HARDENING_MODE_EXTENSIVE']
# and that -D is on the real per-file command line -- counted, not spot-checked:
# 8193 of the 8193 C++ entries in obj-*/clangd/compile_commands.json carry it,
# and none of the 1773 C entries does, which is correct.
#
# AND THIS ROW IS BETTER ON ANDROID THAN ON DESKTOP.  LW-M5-03 ran a real
# --enable-application=browser --target=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu configure: desktop
# resolves to libstdc++ and gets MOZ_STL_HARDENING_FLAGS =
# ['-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS=1'], which covers materially less than libc++'s
# EXTENSIVE mode.  So do not describe this as "the same on both platforms".
ac_add_options --enable-stl-hardening

# --enable-jemalloc: supported; already the default on Android, kept explicit.
# build/moz.configure/memory.configure:6-11 defaults on when
# target.kernel in ("Darwin", "Linux", "WINNT"); an Android triplet canonicalises
# to os="Android", kernel="Linux" (build/moz.configure/init.configure:485-487),
# so the default is already True.  mozjemalloc has explicit Android handling --
# memory/build/moz.build:60.
ac_add_options --enable-jemalloc

# --enable-replace-malloc: supported, and NOT the default on a release build.
# build/moz.configure/memory.configure:38-52 -- replace_malloc_default is only
# True for early-beta-or-earlier, and 153.0.4 is release, so without this line
# MOZ_REPLACE_MALLOC would be off.  Another real parity fix.
# It is gated `when="--enable-jemalloc"` (:49), which is satisfied above.
# Links statically into libmozglue (MOZ_REPLACE_MALLOC_STATIC, :58-65); nothing
# in memory/replace/moz.build excludes Android.
ac_add_options --enable-replace-malloc

# --enable-rust-simd: supported and effective on aarch64.
# js/moz.configure:636-650 -- rust_simd only returns True for
# target.cpu in ("aarch64", "arm", "x86", "x86_64"); aarch64 qualifies, so
# encoding_rs gets its simd-accel feature exactly as on desktop x86_64.
ac_add_options --enable-rust-simd

# --enable-phc: ADDED BY LW-M5-03.  One of the two hardening asymmetries between
# the desktop and Android configurations that a mozconfig can close (the other is
# the LDFLAGS block further down), and it goes the wrong way by default.
# build/moz.configure/memory.configure:74-104.  phc_default returns True for
#   target.cpu in ("x86_64","aarch64") AND (
#       (target.os == "GNU" and target.kernel == "Linux")   <- desktop LibreWolf
#    or  target.kernel == "WINNT" or target.os == "OSX"
#    or (target.os == "Android" and milestone.is_early_beta_or_earlier))
# An Android triplet is os="Android", kernel="Linux", so it misses the first
# arm, and version.android pins a *release* milestone, so it misses the fourth.
# Result: desktop LibreWolf ships MOZ_PHC=1 and Android shipped MOZ_PHC unset --
# confirmed absent from all three built Android config.status files.
# PHC puts a sampled fraction of heap allocations on their own guard-page-flanked
# page and keeps freed ones PROT_NONE, so an overflow or use-after-free on a
# sampled allocation becomes a deterministic SIGSEGV instead of silent
# corruption.  Mozilla gates it on "platforms that have a crash reporter"
# (memory.configure:72-73) for reporting value; we run it with
# --disable-crashreporter, exactly as desktop LibreWolf already does, and keep
# it for the fail-fast behaviour alone.
# Cost, read out of the shipped defaults rather than guessed:
#   * StaticPrefList.yaml:13963-13973 -- memory.phc.size_kb is 1024+128 on a
#     release milestone, i.e. ~1.1 MB of PHC pages per process, not the 16 MB
#     the early-beta branch uses.
#   * xpcom/base/PHCManager.cpp:36-69 -- PHC self-disables (SetPHCState(OnlyFree))
#     unless PR_GetPhysicalMemorySize() >= memory.phc.min_ram_mb, default 8000.
#     On a phone with less than ~8 GB of RAM this option therefore costs code
#     size and nothing else.  Whoever owns settings/*.cfg should decide whether
#     to lower memory.phc.min_ram_mb for Android; that pref is not this file's.
#   * NO codegen change: memory.configure:107-126 implies --enable-frame-pointers
#     only for WINNT/x86, Darwin, or (x86|ppc) without unwind info.  aarch64
#     falls through to False, and MOZ_FRAMEPTR_FLAGS stays
#     ['-fomit-frame-pointer', '-funwind-tables'].
# Android support in the code is real, not incidental: memory/build/PHC.cpp:132-136
# has an #ifdef ANDROID pthread_atfork declaration, mozglue/misc/StackWalk.cpp:54
# counts ANDROID as a MOZ_STACKWALK_SUPPORTS_LINUX platform, and the
# glean::memory_phc::* metrics PHCManager.cpp needs are already generated in the
# Android objdir (obj-*/dist/include/mozilla/glean/XpcomMetrics.h:520-540).
# No LibreWolf patch touches memory/build/ or xpcom/base/.
#
# ONE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE FAT AAR, measured, flagged for LW-M2-03.
# scripts/android-fat-aar.sh:464 generates each per-ABI mozconfig by sed'ing ONLY
# the `ac_add_options --target=` line of this file, so this option reaches the
# armeabi-v7a pass too -- and there `target.cpu` is "arm", which phc_default does
# NOT list, so upstream's default would be OFF.  Confirmed by running
# `./mach configure` with --target=arm-linux-androideabi and this file:
# MOZ_PHC = "1".  That is deliberate: PHC's guard-page mechanism is
# architecture-independent, memory.configure's "we've only been testing on 64bit
# processors" note is about Win32 stack tracing, and a mitigation present on
# arm64-v8a and absent on armeabi-v7a would be a second per-ABI parity split on
# top of the unavoidable one below.  But it IS untested by upstream on 32-bit
# ARM, so if the armv7 pass or its smoke test misbehaves, this is the first thing
# to switch off -- for that ABI only.
ac_add_options --enable-phc

# RLBox wasm sandboxing: ON, by default, and there is deliberately no option
# here.  toolkit/moz.configure:2817-2833 returns the full library list for every
# little-endian target, so aarch64-linux-android gets graphite, ogg, hunspell,
# expat, woff2 and soundtouch sandboxed with no flag from us.  Verified in the
# artefact: MOZ_USING_WASM_SANDBOXING and all six MOZ_WASM_SANDBOXING_* are 1 in
# mozilla-config.h, WASI_SYSROOT resolves, obj-*/security/rlbox/ holds 52
# per-library .wasm modules and an aarch64 rlbox.wasm.o carrying 8907 DISTINCT
# w2c_* symbol names (401 graphite, 182 hunspell, 110 woff2, 88 expat XML_*,
# 85 ogg), and toolkit/library/build/libxul_so.list links it into libxul.
# (SANDBOX-SPIKE.md quotes 8937 for the same object; that is a different filter
# over the same symbol table -- 15788 raw entries, 8907 unique names -- not a
# disagreement.)
#
# DO NOT add --without-wasm-sandboxed-libraries.  Configure suggests it whenever
# anything about the WASI sysroot or the wasm32 compiler-rt is missing, and
# taking that suggestion is a silent security-parity loss: the build goes green
# and six parsers move back into the libxul address space.  The fix is always to
# repair the sysroot -- docs/android/BUILD.md section 4.4 -- never to take the
# suggestion.  LW-M2-01 refused it once and LW-M5-03 refused it again.
# Not pinned with an explicit --with-wasm-sandboxed-libraries=<list> either: the
# default tracks upstream, so a library Mozilla sandboxes in a future ESR is
# picked up automatically, whereas a hardcoded list would silently exclude it.

# The desktop CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS hardening pair, carried verbatim.
#   -ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero : plain clang flag, no target restriction.
#       Note it deliberately *overrides* what --enable-hardening would do:
#       toolchain.configure:2732-2745 only adds -ftrivial-auto-var-init=pattern
#       and only on debug builds, so on a release build these two lines are the
#       ONLY thing zero-initialising locals.  Dropping them would silently lose
#       the mitigation on Android while --enable-hardening still "looks" set.
#       The linux32 carve-out at :2733 is x86-only and does not apply to aarch64.
#   -fwrapv : plain clang flag, target-independent.
#   -Wno-backend-plugin : warning suppression only, target-independent.
export CFLAGS="-ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero -fwrapv -Wno-backend-plugin $CFLAGS"
export CXXFLAGS="-ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero -fwrapv -Wno-backend-plugin $CXXFLAGS"

# LDFLAGS -- ADDED BY LW-M5-03.  Android-only, because this is the one place the
# Android toolchain is measurably WEAKER than the desktop one and it is not our
# doing: the NDK's clang driver passes --no-rosegment to ld.lld unconditionally
# (visible in `clang -### --target=aarch64-linux-android26 -shared`).  The result
# is a single R+E PT_LOAD covering .rodata, .eh_frame, .eh_frame_hdr,
# .gcc_except_table, .dynstr, .rela.* AND .text -- i.e. read-only DATA mapped
# executable.  Measured on our own libxul.so: the R+E segment is 151,379,792
# bytes of which only 109,086,052 are code, so 42,290,043 bytes (40.3 MiB,
# 27.9%) of non-code was executable.  The SAME lld targeting
# x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu emits a separate read-only segment, so desktop
# LibreWolf already ships the layout this line restores.
#
# Measured cost: zero.  With the flag, libxul.so is 4 LOAD segments
# (R 42,293,728 / R+E 109,086,064 / RW / RW), executable-mapped non-code is 0
# bytes, and the file is byte-for-byte the SAME SIZE as without it -- the segment
# boundary already fell on the 16 KB alignment.  ./mach build exits 0.
#
# Why it is safe to pass here rather than in a CFLAGS-style hack:
#   * CORRECTION (skeptical verification of LW-M5-03): the original claim here --
#     that flags.configure routes user link flags through
#     check_and_add_linker_flag, so a future NDK rejecting the flag would make
#     configure DROP it rather than fail -- is FALSE, and it was marked (M) for
#     measured.  build/moz.configure/flags.configure:46-47 extends
#     os.environ["LDFLAGS"] straight into env_flags: a raw passthrough with no
#     link test.  check_and_add_linker_flag is used only for configure's OWN
#     flags (:354-361, -Wl,-z,relro and friends).
#     So this flag is NOT self-disabling.  If a future NDK rejects it the build
#     FAILS, loudly, at link time.  That is an acceptable failure mode -- loud
#     beats a silently dropped hardening flag -- but it is a rebase hazard and
#     must be re-checked, not assumed away.  The measurement that DOES hold is
#     the one that matters: with this flag, ./mach build exits 0 on NDK r29
#     clang 21.0.0 and the segment boundary falls on the 16 KB alignment.
#   * config/rules.mk:493-497 links the RLBox .wasm archive with $(WASM_CXX) and
#     its own flag list; wasm-ld never sees LDFLAGS.
#   * libxul.so on Android is linked through relrhack (RELRHACK=1), which rewrites
#     program headers.  Verified in the resulting ELF: DT_RELR/DT_RELRSZ are
#     unchanged at 101,704 bytes, DT_INIT still points into the code segment, and
#     every ALLOC section is covered by a LOAD segment.
#   * MOZ_LINKER=1 on Android, and mozglue/linker/CustomElf.cpp:100-120 collects
#     PT_LOADs into a vector -- no fixed segment count.
# NOT verified: nobody has run this binary.  A segment layout the loader rejects
# fails loudly at process start, so ./scripts/android-smoke.sh owns the final
# word.  If it regresses, delete this line -- that is the whole revert.
export LDFLAGS="-Wl,--rosegment $LDFLAGS"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telemetry / data-reporting kill switches
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same option names as desktop -- all three are toolkit-level, not browser-level.
#
# MOZ_TELEMETRY_REPORTING: toolkit/moz.configure:1123-1127, an env-style option
# whose default is `mozilla_official`.  Because we set MOZILLA_OFFICIAL=1 below,
# this empty override is load-bearing: without it telemetry reporting would be
# compiled IN.  Mozilla themselves use the same trick in
# mobile/android/config/mozconfigs/android-x86_64/debug-fuzzing:4.
export MOZ_TELEMETRY_REPORTING=

# MOZ_REQUIRE_SIGNING: toolkit/moz.configure:2040-2047, defaults to True on
# release/non-ESR.  Empty value disables the add-on signing requirement, which
# is what upstream Android does too (mobile/android/config/mozconfigs/
# common.override:8).
export MOZ_REQUIRE_SIGNING=

# Kept identical to desktop.  IMPORTANT: these are make-level only.
# python/mozbuild/mozbuild/mozconfig.py:245-272 routes every `mk_add_options
# NAME=VALUE` into result["make_extra"]; it never reaches configure's
# environment.  That matters here, because mobile/android/moz.configure:132
# does `imply_option("MOZ_SERVICES_HEALTHREPORT", True)` -- if these lines were
# `export`ed instead, configure would die on the implied/explicit conflict.
# (browser/moz.configure:6 does the same imply on desktop, which is why the
# desktop config gets away with it too.)  So: carried for parity, but do not
# mistake them for an actual off switch -- MOZ_DATA_REPORTING is computed at
# toolkit/moz.configure:3493-3507 from telemetry|healthreport|crashreporter|
# normandy, and it is --disable-crashreporter plus the empty
# MOZ_TELEMETRY_REPORTING above that do the real work.
mk_add_options MOZ_CRASHREPORTER=0
mk_add_options MOZ_DATA_REPORTING=0
mk_add_options MOZ_SERVICES_HEALTHREPORT=0
mk_add_options MOZ_TELEMETRY_REPORTING=0

# The Android-only telemetry stack (Glean, Adjust, Play Integrity/Firebase)
# lives in the Fenix/android-components Gradle layer and is NOT reachable from
# a mozconfig option.  It is removed by LW-M4-01, LW-M4-02 and LW-M4-05.
# --enable-geckoview-lite (mobile/android/moz.configure:78-83) would drop Glean
# from GeckoView, but Fenix references Glean classes directly, so turning it on
# here breaks the APK build.  Left off deliberately; revisit under LW-M4-01.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Official build / naming
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
export MOZILLA_OFFICIAL=1

# --with-app-name is a real, target-independent option (js/moz.configure:19-24,
# env MOZ_APP_NAME).  On Android it is far less load-bearing than on desktop:
# the only two consumers in mobile/ are
#   mobile/android/installer/Makefile.in:32 (-DMOZ_APP_NAME, unused by
#     package-manifest.in) and
#   mobile/android/geckoview/build.gradle:43 (a BuildConfig string field).
# There is no `librewolf` binary on Android and no profile path derived from it.
# Carried anyway for parity and for what about:buildconfig reports.
ac_add_options --with-app-name=librewolf

# NOT set: MOZ_APP_BASENAME.  Defaults to "Fennec" on Android
# (build/moz.configure/init.configure:1283-1290) rather than desktop's
# "Firefox".  Changing it is display-name/branding work -> LW-M4-07.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Branding  -- placeholder, real work is LW-M4-07
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The desktop LibreWolf branding directory (the one assets/mozconfig points at,
# under the desktop "browser" tree) DOES NOT EXIST for Android and must never
# be referenced here: mobile/android/confvars.sh:5-6 sets
#   MOZ_BRANDING_DIRECTORY=mobile/android/branding/unofficial
#   MOZ_OFFICIAL_BRANDING_DIRECTORY=mobile/android/branding/official
# and the only branding dirs that exist are mobile/android/branding/{beta,
# nightly,official,unofficial}.  Pointing --with-branding at a desktop path
# would break mobile/android/locales/Makefile.in:7-9, which pulls
# $(MOZ_BRANDING_DIRECTORY)/Makefile and .../locales/Makefile into the build.
#
# TODO(LW-M4-07): create mobile/android/branding/librewolf (configure.sh with
# MOZ_APP_DISPLAYNAME, content/, locales/, moz.build -- copy the shape of
# mobile/android/branding/unofficial/) and point this line at it.  Until then
# we use the upstream unofficial branding, which is the non-trademarked one
# (its configure.sh sets MOZ_APP_DISPLAYNAME="Fennec", not "Firefox").
# Do NOT switch to --enable-official-branding: that is Mozilla trademark
# material (toolkit/moz.configure:3208-3215).
ac_add_options --with-branding=mobile/android/branding/unofficial


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Localisation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --with-l10n-base is toolkit-level (toolkit/moz.configure:493-514), not
# browser-only, and mobile/android DOES use it: mobile/android/locales/l10n.toml
# resolves every path through `l = "{l10n_base}/{locale}/"`, which is exactly
# the per-locale layout scripts/librewolf-patches.py:288 unpacks into lw/l10n
# from the pinned mozilla-l10n/firefox-l10n archive.  So the desktop value is
# still correct here.  ($topsrcdir rather than $PWD: identical in practice --
# the mozconfig is sourced with cwd == topsrcdir -- but it does not depend on
# the caller's working directory.)
#
# CAVEAT, and it is a big one: this only covers the *Gecko/GeckoView* strings,
# and on Android those are almost nothing -- mobile/android/locales/en-US
# contains just geckoViewConsole.ftl and aboutConfig.ftl.  The entire Fenix UI
# is localised through a completely different mechanism:
# mobile/android/fenix/l10n.toml has `basepath = "."` and its values-<locale>/
# strings.xml files are synced into the tree from mozilla-l10n/android-l10n by
# Mozilla automation.  --with-l10n-base has no effect on them, and neither does
# the l10n pin in assets/l10n-pin.txt.  Whoever ends up owning Android string
# overrides (the "Firefox" -> "LibreWolf" sed at librewolf-patches.py:290-292
# only touches appstrings.properties) needs a separate mechanism.  Not solved
# by this file; flagged for LW-M4-07 / LW-M5-03.
ac_add_options --with-l10n-base="$topsrcdir/lw/l10n"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DROPPED / CHANGED FROM DESKTOP  -- worked through by LW-M5-03
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HARDENING OPTIONS DROPPED: none.  All eight of --enable-hardening,
# --enable-stl-hardening, --enable-jemalloc, --enable-replace-malloc,
# --enable-rust-simd, -ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero, -fwrapv and
# -Wno-backend-plugin are present above and, since LW-M5-03, verified IN EFFECT
# on aarch64-linux-android from the built ELF and the real compiler command
# lines -- not merely "supported".
#
# HARDENING ADDED by LW-M5-03 -- the two asymmetries a mozconfig can close:
#   1. --enable-phc.  Desktop gets MOZ_PHC from configure's default (verified in
#      a real x86_64-pc-linux-gnu config.status) and Android did not.
#   2. export LDFLAGS="-Wl,--rosegment ...".  The NDK clang driver passes
#      --no-rosegment, so 42,290,043 bytes of read-only DATA in libxul.so were
#      mapped executable; desktop's lld does not do this and needs no flag.
#      After: 0 bytes, same file size.
#
# ONE ROW WHERE ANDROID IS AHEAD OF DESKTOP: STL hardening.  Android gets
# _LIBCPP_HARDENING_MODE_EXTENSIVE, desktop gets _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS=1.
#
# ONE RESIDUAL GAP NO FLAG CAN CLOSE: MOZ_SANDBOX is 1 on desktop and absent on
# every Android target.  See docs/android/SANDBOX-SPIKE.md (LW-M5-07).
#
# ONE PER-ABI SPLIT NO FLAG CAN CLOSE: armeabi-v7a gets no
# -fstack-clash-protection.  See the --enable-hardening block above.
#
# Considered and deliberately NOT added -- full reasoning, measurements and
# residual gaps in docs/android/parity/hardening-flags.md:
#   -mbranch-protection=pac-ret / =standard (BTI), -fsanitize=shadow-call-stack,
#   -fzero-call-used-regs, -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=3, -fstrict-flex-arrays>1,
#   --enable-sandbox, --enable-lto + CFI, MTE.
#
# Non-hardening desktop lines not carried, with reasons:
#
# 1. ac_add_options --enable-application=browser
#    -> replaced by --enable-application=mobile/android.  Obviously.
#
# 2. ac_add_options --disable-default-browser-agent
#    -> DROPPED.  build/moz.configure/update-programs.configure:228-246: the
#       default is `target.os == "WINNT" and build_project == "browser"`, i.e.
#       already False for us, and set_config is gated `when=target_is_windows`.
#       The option is declared unconditionally so passing it would not actually
#       error, but it is dead weight on Android and the board explicitly asked
#       for desktop-only options to stay out.
#
# 3. the desktop --with-branding line (it names a directory in the desktop
#    "browser" tree, which does not exist for Android)
#    -> REPLACED with mobile/android/branding/unofficial; see the branding
#       block above.  Placeholder pending LW-M4-07.
#
# 4. export MOZ_APP_REMOTINGNAME=LibreWolf
#    -> DROPPED.  toolkit/moz.configure:3336-3354; the value only reaches
#       build/application.ini.in:21 (RemotingName) and
#       toolkit/xre/nsAppRunner.cpp:6397, both of which implement the
#       X11/DBus single-instance remoting protocol.  Android has no XRE
#       remoting path -- GeckoView is started by the Android framework -- so
#       setting it changes nothing.  Harmless but meaningless; left out rather
#       than carried as noise.  If a future task finds it in about:buildconfig
#       output and wants it for cosmetic consistency, re-adding is safe.
#
# 5. mk_add_options MOZ_* lines
#    -> KEPT verbatim, but see the note above: they are make-level only and
#       never reach configure.  This is true on desktop too.
#
# Android-only additions that have no desktop counterpart:
#   --target=aarch64-linux-android, --enable-appservices-in-tree,
#   --with-android-sdk / --with-android-ndk / --with-java-bin-path /
#   --with-gradle (all env-driven, LW-M0-06), --disable-updater (explicit).
#
# Added by LW-M2-04, which wired up the APK build (mobile/android/moz.configure:85-100
# selects the Fenix Gradle task set).  It was deliberately withheld through M0/M1
# so those milestones stayed a GeckoView-only build and a failure there could not
# be confused with a Fenix-stage failure.  That reason has expired.
#
# The APK LW-M2-04 verified was built from a private copy of this file carrying
# this line; the repo file was left without it, so `make android-apk` died at
# preflight and the deliverable was not reproducible from the repository. Caught
# by the skeptical verification of LW-M2-04.
ac_add_options --enable-android-subproject=fenix
